-- title:
--   $R(q^s)=\alpha\,R_s(q^s)$
-- statement:
--   Let $q^s$ be feasible for Problem (2) and induce a strongly connected network, and let $\pi$ be a stationary distribution of the chain of the static policy. Then for every location $i$
--
--   $$
--   R(q^s,\pi)=\sum_{x\in S}\pi(x)\sum_{(k,j)\in\mathcal A}\Lambda_{kj}q_{kj}(x)I_{kj}(q_{kj}(x))=\alpha_i\,R_s(q^s),
--   $$
--
--   where $\alpha_i$ is the availability of location $i$. This is the paper's identity $R(q^s)/R_s(q^s)=\alpha$, which reduces the performance of the static policy to the availability.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: trip durations i.i.d. with a general distribution with rational Laplace transform and mean 1/μij, represented by a network of exponential stages (p. 4) → a phase-type law per pair: finitely many stages, entry probabilities β (total ≤ 1; the defect is a zero-length trip), rates r between stages, exit rates e, an exit reachable from every stage; 1/μij = β(−T)⁻¹𝟙; reason: the paper's state counts vehicles at each stage of the transit queue (footnote 1), so the model exists exactly for laws with such a stage network, i.e. phase-type laws; the general (not only Coxian) form is taken
--   - encoding: demand, prices, rewards and travel data on the pairs (i,j) ∈ 𝒜 (Λij > 0) → all of them given on every pair of locations; reason: pairs with Λij = 0 enter every rate, sum and constraint with factor 0; no instance changes
--   - encoding: prices pij with acceptance fractions qij = 1 − Fij(pij) (p. 5) → the fractions q ∈ [0,1] and the per-trip rewards Iij(q) as primitives, q·Iij(q) concave on [0,1]; reason: the paper reformulates the policy in terms of the quantiles q (p. 5); concavity of q·I is its only requirement
--   - generalization: q^s an optimal solution of Problem (2) with Δ ≥ K → q^s feasible for Problem (2) (bounds, balanced flows, workload ≤ Δ), any Δ; reason: the argument uses only that the flows of q^s are balanced and q^s ∈ [0,1]; the paper's optimal q^s is a special case
--   - generalization: the steady-state probability π of the static chain → every stationary distribution π of the static chain; reason: the paper's π is one of them (it is unique, milestone p. 6); nothing rests on uniqueness
--   - reading: "q^s induces a strongly connected network" (p. 6) → FlowStronglyConnected: in the digraph on all locations with an arc a → b whenever Λab q^s_ab > 0, every location reaches every location; reason: the network induced by a price vector is the graph of its positive demand flows; with one location the condition holds trivially
--   - encoding: R(q^s)/Rs(q^s) = α → R(q^s, π) = α_i · Rs(q^s) for every location i; reason: multiplicative form; avoids dividing by Rs(q^s), which may be 0
--   - generalization: the network generated by 𝒩 and 𝒜 is strongly connected (p. 4, without loss of generality) → not assumed separately; reason: it follows from the strong connectivity of the flow of q^s, whose arcs lie in 𝒜; the paper's instances are a special case
-- source:
--   Benjaafar, Shen, Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks, SSRN 3998297 (version of 2023-01-10), https://ssrn.com/abstract=3998297, proof of Theorem 1, pp. 7–8 (unnumbered display), via (3), p. 6

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_IsFeasible2
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_staticPolicy
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_IsStationary
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_FlowStronglyConnected
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_avail
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_reward
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_Rs

namespace VehShare.SeqBounds

/-- **`R(q^s)/R_s(q^s) = α`.**
Benjaafar, Shen, *Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks*, SSRN 3998297
(version of 2023-01-10), proof of Theorem 1, pp. 7–8 (unnumbered display, via (3), p. 6).

Let `q^s` be feasible for Problem (2) and induce a strongly connected network. For every stationary
distribution `π` of the static chain and every location `i`,
`R(q^s, π) = α_i · R_s(q^s)`.

**Formalization Note.** Encoding 4: the ratio `R(q^s)/R_s(q^s) = α` is stated multiplicatively, so
no division by `R_s(q^s)` occurs. Only feasibility of `q^s` is used, with any `Δ` (a
generalization of the paper's optimal `q^s`). -/
theorem reward_eq_avail_mul_Rs (P : Instance) (K : ℕ) (Δ : ℝ) (qs : P.L → P.L → ℝ)
    (hqs : IsFeasible2 P Δ qs) (hsc : FlowStronglyConnected P qs)
    (π : State P K → ℝ) (hπ : IsStationary (staticPolicy P K qs hqs.1) π) (i : P.L) :
    reward (staticPolicy P K qs hqs.1) π = avail π i * Rs P qs := by sorry

end VehShare.SeqBounds
